From 7cd67ca3f5be61e3c4c846af346fd43ba62325f1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Neivin Detlefsen <neivin.detlefsen@gmx.de>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 22:39:36 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 13/13] fix: run the spc from reset and end the first frame at
 the first vblank

Two properties of the APU frame timeline put the SPC behind the
hardware for the whole run.

The guest-to-SPC mapping was only set by apu_schedulePortWrite, at the
CPU's first write to $2140-$2143. Until then apu_runToGuestCycle
returned at once, so neither port reads nor the frame-boundary sync ran
the SPC. RtlRunFrame now maps the start of its frame whenever nothing
is mapped (apu_anchorGuestTime): the first frame after reset, or the
frame after an HLE upload cleared the port schedule. An existing
mapping stays, and the callback-ahead rebase in apu_schedulePortWrite
is unchanged. The shared-clock co-sim keeps its touch-only pacing.

With extended frame timing every frame counted at least a whole field
(17088 SPC cycles). The first frame of the beam frame driver runs from
reset to V=225, 306902 master clocks or 14674 SPC cycles, and gained
2413. The first frame after reset now has the reset-to-first-vblank
span as its minimum; WAI still advances a whole field from the second
frame on.

FF6 writes first about 5888 SPC cycles into its first frame. Net, its
SPC ran 3475 cycles (about 53 lines) behind the CPU from the first
frame cut on; every SPC handshake wait then ended at a different point
inside the frame than on hardware.

Regression: tests/audio/apu_frame_timeline_test.c drives RtlRunFrame
from reset (7 FAIL before the fix: SPC at 0 without a port write,
11200 instead of 14674 at the first cut, first write scheduled at 0
instead of 5888), and apu_port_guest_time_test.c checks the mapping
and the first-frame clock (3 FAIL before).
